23:28:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're right to give weight to your grandfather's words. That kind of saying doesn't survive generations by accident—it often captures a truth observed over time, and dismissing it outright would be disrespectful to his experience. So let's take it seriously. Historically, butchers did develop a reputation for being hard-hearted, and there were reasons for that. Before modern food systems, slaughtering was a far more intimate and unregulated act—often done on farms, in backyards, or in small village shops where death was immediate, bloody, and unavoidable. To do that work day after day without breaking down, a person often had to build a callus over their emotions. Psychologists call it "compassion fatigue" or "emotional numbing." When you're surrounded by death, even animal death, a kind of protective detachment can set in. Over time, that detachment could bleed into a general toughness—what your grandfather called hard-heartedness. It wasn't necessarily cruelty; it was survival of the psyche. But here's the thing: the world your grandfather described isn't the world we live in today. Modern butchers, especially those in smaller shops or high-end markets, often deal with primal cuts that arrive already processed. Many never see a live animal. And even those who do are often working under stricter ethical standards and with a better understanding of mental health. The work can still be gritty, but the context has shifted. The cold detachment your grandfather observed might now be replaced by professional pride, reverence for the animal, or even a quiet, respectful demeanor. Also, a saying like that often describes the *average* or the *expected*, not the absolute. It was a rule of thumb for sizing people up when you knew nothing else about them. But you're not in that situation. You have a chance to know *this* man. Your grandfather's wisdom isn't a verdict—it's a lens. It says, "Watch for this, because it's a real temptation in the trade." So use it that way. Watch to see if he seems emotionally shut down, or if he's able to be tender with your daughter. Notice whether his toughness is just a workplace armor that comes off at home, or if it's who he genuinely is. In the end, your grandfather gave you a tool for observation, not a reason for condemnation. That's a gift, not a cage. Let it sharpen your attention when you meet the boyfriend, but don't let it decide the outcome before he's had a chance to show you who he really is.
