23:29:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you. And you're not wrong to feel that way—there's a straightforward logic to it that's hard to argue with. Repeated exposure to something most people find emotionally difficult—blood, mortality, the physical disassembly of a body—does change a person. It has to. The first time someone does that work, it's probably shocking. By the hundredth time, it's Tuesday. That's not a moral failing; it's human adaptation. And yes, that adaptation can look and feel like hardening. The butcher might become desensitized in ways that make others uncomfortable. He might joke about things that would make your wife turn pale. He might carry himself with a kind of stoic bluntness that reads as cold. So your fundamental point stands: the work leaves a mark. I'm not arguing against that. But here's the question worth sitting with: *What kind* of mark, and does it make him a bad partner to your daughter? There's a difference between a man who has learned to compartmentalize—to do hard things at work and then come home and be soft with the people he loves—and a man whose heart has genuinely gone cold. The first kind of hardening is functional. It's a tool he puts on and takes off. Many professions demand it: soldiers, surgeons, crime scene cleaners. They see things we wouldn't want to see, and they develop a necessary distance to survive. But that distance can coexist with deep tenderness in their private lives. In fact, some of the gentlest fathers and husbands I've met have been men whose jobs exposed them to constant harshness. They've learned to value softness precisely because they know its opposite so well. The second kind—where the hardening seeps into every part of a person—is a real risk. It happens when someone doesn't have an outlet, or when the work wears them down over decades without balance. And that's worth watching for. No one should dismiss that possibility. But I'd gently push back on "that's really all there is to it." Because it's not all that matters, is it? A person is more than their occupation's psychological side effects. He's also the way he treats your daughter when she's had a bad day. The way he speaks to her when they disagree. Whether he shows up for her, whether he's patient with her mother's unease, whether he can sit with you and have a real conversation or just grunts and deflects. So let me meet you where you are: yes, the work probably does toughen a person. It likely changes their relationship with mortality and physicality in ways most of us don't experience. That's real. But whether that toughness translates into hardness *of heart*, and whether that hardness affects his capacity to love your daughter well—those are separate questions. And they can only be answered by looking at the man, not just his apron.
