23:30:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I understand the impulse. Completely. When it's your daughter—when it's someone you love more than yourself—the math changes. You're not just weighing probabilities anymore, you're imagining worst-case scenarios at 3 a.m. And in that light, assuming the worst can feel like the only responsible move. If you're already braced for trouble, then trouble can't surprise you. It's a kind of emotional insurance. But I want to be honest with you about the hidden cost of that insurance, because it's steeper than it looks. If you assume the worst about this man based solely on his trade, you're not just protecting yourselves—you're also building a wall between you and your daughter. She'll feel it. She'll sense the suspicion, the guardedness, the silent verdict you've already passed on someone she cares about. And over time, that can push her away. It can make her feel like she has to choose between her family and her partner, or like she can't bring him around. That's a painful rift, and it often starts with a parent's well-intentioned wariness. There's also a subtler risk: If you've already decided he's hard-hearted, you'll unconsciously look for evidence to confirm it. Every quiet moment will seem like coldness. Every dry joke will sound cruel. Every shop story will be proof of brutality. You might miss the ways he's actually gentle, because you've already written them off as exceptions or an act. And if he turns out to be a truly good man, you'll have damaged the relationship before it ever had a chance. Now, does that mean you should throw caution to the wind? No. There's a wide middle ground between assuming the worst and being naive. You can be watchful without being hostile. You can pay close attention to how he treats your daughter, how he handles frustration, whether he seems capable of warmth and vulnerability, without needing him to prove his worth on day one. You can give him enough rope to show you who he is, while still keeping your eyes open. Think of it this way: Your real safeguard isn't a blanket assumption about butchers. It's your ongoing presence in your daughter's life. It's the fact that she trusts you enough to bring him around in the first place. It's the conversations you'll have with her privately, where you can ask gentle questions and share observations if something genuinely concerns you. That's a much stronger protection than preemptive judgment, because it's based on reality, not fear. Assuming the worst feels safer, but it's actually more fragile. It's brittle. It breaks under the weight of real relationships. The harder path—the one that takes courage—is to stay open, stay observant, and trust that if there's real trouble, you'll see it clearly enough to act. And if there's no trouble, you won't have poisoned something good out of fear.
